<div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: #ffffff; padding: 20px; text-align: center; border-radius: 8px;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # Language Is an Important Skill – Lesson # 1</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 1</p></td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Listening</p></td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Grade:</b> 12th Grade (CEFR: B1.3)</p></td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Scenario:</b> My Multilingual Self</p></td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Theme:</b> Language Is an Important Skill</p></td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</p></td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</p></td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Listening: Receptive: Demonstrate comprehension of extended discussions on abstract topics. Interactive: Describe the storyline of an audio or visual narrative.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Listening: Can demonstrate comprehension of an extended discussion on multilingualism in Panama's service sector (e.g., "The speaker said that being bilingual is essential for engineers working on international infrastructure projects, as they need to communicate with foreign clients and partners." "Another point mentioned was that multilingualism in logistics companies operating in the Panama Canal Zone improves coordination with global stakeholders, opening doors to higher-level management positions.")<br>Can describe the storyline of an audio narrative about a bilingual person in Panama, noting how multilingualism was helpful (e.g., "In the story, the shop owner said that speaking both Spanish and English helped attract more tourists.")<br>Can summarize an audio segment on multilingual education in Panama, highlighting challenges and solutions (e.g., "The speaker said that students in Bocas del Toro benefit from practicing English with tourists, even though access to formal language courses can be limited.")</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 25px;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; color: #1a5276; width: 35%;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Stage & Description</p></th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; color: #1a5276; width: 50%;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Activities & Teacher Actions</p></th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; color: #1a5276; width: 15%;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Estimated Date and Time</p></th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0; color: #1a5276;"><b>STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task<br>(Engagement, Modeling and Clarification)</b></p></td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher begins by showing a short video clip or an image collage featuring people speaking multiple languages in different contexts within Panama (e.g., Canal Zone, tourism, indigenous communities). Students discuss in pairs: "What <b>skills</b> do these people demonstrate? Why might <b>multilingualism</b> be important for their <b>career opportunities</b>?" The teacher encourages students to share initial thoughts, linking to the theme "Language Is an Important Skill."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher models responses like, "I believe that strong <b>communication skills</b> are essential," and "They possess high language <b>proficiency</b>." The teacher models the pronunciation of key vocabulary words such as "multilingualism," "opportunities," and "proficiency," emphasizing word stress. The teacher also demonstrates how to use the target grammar structure: "She <b>said that multilingualism opens</b> many doors."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b> The teacher checks understanding of new concepts.
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"If someone speaks three languages fluently, are they bilingual or <b>multilingual</b>?" (Expected answer: Multilingual)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"If a company <b>prioritizes</b> hiring people with strong English, what does that mean they consider important?" (Expected answer: English language ability)</li>
                </ul>
                The teacher provides examples: "Being fluent in Spanish and English can significantly enhance your <b>career opportunities</b> in the Panamanian service sector."</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">8 min</p></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0; color: #1a5276;"><b>STAGE 2 — Presentation</b></p></td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "My Multilingual Self".</b> The teacher presents an authentic audio interview segment (approximately 3-4 minutes) from a Panamanian podcast or radio show discussing the impact of <b>multilingualism</b> in Panama's service sector and challenges/solutions in multilingual <b>education</b>. The audio features different speakers sharing their opinions and predictions.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> Audio interview segment (MP3 file) from a local Panamanian podcast on "Language Skills for <b>Success</b> in Panama." The register is semi-formal, featuring experts and professionals discussing their experiences and observations. The focus is on complex ideas related to <b>business</b>, <b>career opportunities</b>, and <b>education</b>.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Interprets details and main ideas from extended audio discussions on abstract topics, specifically regarding the importance of language <b>proficiency</b> and <b>communication</b> in professional contexts.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b> Students listen to the audio twice.</p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>First listening (Gist):</b> Students identify the main topic of discussion and who the speakers are (e.g., "The main topic is about the benefits of <b>multilingualism</b> in Panama," "One speaker is an expert in <b>education</b>."). They should answer: "What general opinions are shared about language in Panama?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Second listening (Specific details):</b> Students complete a True/False exercise or information matching task based on statements related to the audio. For example:
                        <ul>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"The speaker said that being <b>bilingual</b> is essential for engineers working on international infrastructure projects." (True)</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Another point mentioned was that <b>multilingualism</b> in logistics companies operating in the Panama Canal Zone improves coordination." (True)</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"The audio argued that learning an indigenous <b>language</b> is not a valuable <b>skill</b> for national <b>business</b>." (False)</li>
                        </ul>
                    Students will also note down any predictions made about future job market trends related to language <b>skills</b>, like "Companies <b>will prioritize</b> hiring <b>bilingual</b> employees in the future."</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">12 min</p></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0; color: #1a5276;"><b>STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b></p></td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Accuracy focus on Listening.</b> This stage reinforces comprehension and introduces active use of grammar and vocabulary.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Students practice identifying and reporting information, making predictions, and discussing abilities using <b>Reported speech</b> ("She said that..."), <b>Future simple</b> ("Companies will prioritize..."), and <b>Modals of ability</b> ("You can..."). They will actively use vocabulary: <b>multilingualism, language, proficiency, communication, career, opportunities, business, education, fluency, skill, said, argued, believe, prioritize, offer, communicate, become, excel, enhance, learn, bilingual, multilingual, proficient, effective, international, diverse, multicultural, necessary, fluent</b>. Pronunciation focus on /s/ in "<b>success</b>" and /z/ in "<b>citizens</b>", and word stress in "multilingualism," "multicultural."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">1. <b>Controlled Practice (Listening Gap-Fill):</b> Students listen to specific short segments of the audio again. They complete sentences on a worksheet using <b>reported speech</b> or the <b>future simple</b> based on what they hear. For example: "The business leader _______________ (said/argued) that companies _______________ (will/can) prioritize hiring <b>proficient</b> speakers." Or "He _______________ (said) that strong <b>communication skills</b> _______________ (enhance) <b>career opportunities</b>." The teacher pauses the audio after each segment for students to write their answers.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">2. <b>Semi-Controlled Practice (Sentence Transformation & Modals):</b> The teacher provides sentences from the audio and asks students to rephrase them using different verbs or focusing on <b>modals of ability</b>. For instance, "Being <b>multilingual</b> helps you connect with diverse cultures." can be transformed into "You <b>can</b> connect with <b>diverse</b> cultures if you are <b>multilingual</b>." Or "<b>Multilingualism</b> allows us to connect with people from different cultures and shows respect for their background." Students practice this in pairs, comparing their transformations and practicing pronunciation of /s/ and /z/.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">3. <b>Pair Interaction (Audio Segment Summary):</b> Students work in pairs. Each pair is assigned a different 30-second segment from the audio. They listen carefully and then summarize the key points of their segment to their partner, focusing on using <b>reported speech</b> and incorporating at least three target vocabulary words. Example: "My segment discussed how <b>multilingualism enhances business opportunities</b>, and the speaker <b>said that</b> being <b>proficient</b> in several <b>languages</b> is becoming <b>necessary</b> for many roles." They also discuss how "If we all learn even a little of each other's <b>languages</b>, we <b>can</b> make our community more <b>inclusive</b> and understanding."</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">12 min</p></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0; color: #1a5276;"><b>STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b></p></td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "My Multilingual Self".</b> Students engage in a collaborative task that requires them to apply their listening comprehension and integrate the target language.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format: Group Summary and Discussion.</b> Students are divided into small groups of 3-4. They listen to a new, short audio narrative (approx. 2 minutes) about a <b>bilingual</b> person in Panama describing how their <b>multilingualism</b> was helpful in a specific situation (e.g., a shop owner attracting tourists, a student in Bocas del Toro practicing English). Each group's task is to collaboratively describe the storyline of the audio narrative and summarize how <b>multilingualism</b> was beneficial, then discuss potential future implications.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">1. <b>Listen and Note-take:</b> Students listen to the audio narrative twice, taking notes on the main characters, setting, and key events, focusing on instances where language <b>proficiency</b> was helpful.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">2. <b>Collaborative Storyline Description:</b> In their groups, students describe the storyline, making sure to use <b>reported speech</b> to convey what the character "<b>said</b>" or "<b>argued</b>." For example: "In the story, the shop owner <b>said that</b> speaking both Spanish and English helped attract more tourists." They discuss how the character used their language <b>skill</b>.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">3. <b>Summarize and Predict:</b> Groups then summarize how <b>multilingualism</b> was helpful in the narrative. They extend this by predicting how such <b>skills will</b> likely open up more <b>career opportunities</b> in the future, using the future simple. They also consider culturally sensitive advice, such as: "<b>Multilingualism</b> allows us to connect with people from different cultures and shows respect for their background."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">4. <b>Group Share:</b> Each group briefly presents their storyline description and summary to the class. The teacher encourages cross-group questions and discussions, emphasizing pronunciation of /s/ and /z/ (e.g., "success," "citizens").</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">8 min</p></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0; color: #1a5276;"><b>STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b></p></td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative assessment strategy: Teacher Observation Checklist and Quick Written Summary.</b> The teacher uses a checklist during the group share-out (Stage 4) to note students' ability to comprehend the audio narrative, describe its storyline, and use target grammar and vocabulary. Additionally, students complete a short written task.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Individually, students write a 2-3 sentence summary of the audio narrative they listened to in Stage 4, focusing on how <b>multilingualism</b> was helpful. They should aim to use at least one instance of <b>reported speech</b> and two target vocabulary words (e.g., <b>multilingualism, career opportunities, communication, skill</b>).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Listening Comprehension:</b> Accurately describes the storyline and key points from the audio narrative.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Use of Target Vocabulary:</b> Appropriately integrates at least two target vocabulary words (e.g., <b>multilingualism, proficiency, opportunities</b>).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Grammar Accuracy:</b> Correctly uses <b>reported speech</b> or <b>future simple</b> when summarizing or describing predictions.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Clarity and Cohesion:</b> The summary is clear and easy to understand.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Intelligible Pronunciation:</b> (Observed during Stage 4 sharing) Clear pronunciation, especially of /s/ and /z/ sounds.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students answer the following questions on a small slip of paper:</p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was one new idea you learned about the importance of <b>multilingualism</b> in Panama today?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How confident are you now in understanding extended discussions in English (1-5, 5 being very confident)?"</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5 min</p></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0; color: #1a5276;"><b>STAGE 6 — Reflection</b></p></td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was the most challenging part of listening to the discussions today, and what strategy did you use or could you use next time to improve your comprehension?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How do you think improving your <b>listening skills</b> in English <b>will enhance</b> your own <b>career opportunities</b> in the future?"</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What worked well in this lesson to engage students and promote listening comprehension? (e.g., "The varied audio inputs kept students engaged, and the pair-work during practice helped scaffold understanding of complex topics.")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What adjustments or scaffolds are needed for students who struggled with the pace or abstract nature of the audio content? (e.g., "Consider providing a short glossary of academic terms before listening next time, or offering a transcript for a third listening to support weaker listeners.")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">How can the <b>vocabulary</b> and <b>grammar</b> introduced today be reinforced in the next lesson?</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson in the theme sequence:</b> The core <b>vocabulary</b> (e.g., <b>multilingualism, proficiency, communication, opportunities</b>) and grammatical structures (<b>reported speech</b>) introduced today <b>will</b> be revisited and reinforced in Lesson 2, which <b>will focus</b> on reading comprehension using a written text about similar aspects of language <b>skills</b> in Panama. This <b>will offer</b> students another modality to engage with the concepts and deepen their understanding.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5 min</p></td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>